{
  "evals": [
    {
      "id": "tao-run-on-docker-four-verb-lifecycle",
      "prompt": "Run a short-lived container job on local Docker end to end using the `tao-run-on-docker` skill, and report its final state.\n\nUse a tiny public image — `busybox:1.36` is fine. This eval checks the platform execution contract (job lifecycle), NOT model training, so **do not** pull a TAO image and do not request a GPU.\n\nMake the container print a recognizable token and stay alive briefly, e.g.:\n\n    sh -c 'echo tao-smoke-alive; sleep 30'\n\nWalk all four verbs the skill documents, in order:\n\n1. **submit** — lint the assembled command, open the job record to mint `$JOB_ID` BEFORE launching, launch the container named after `$JOB_ID`, then mark the record RUNNING with the container id as `--backend-ref`.\n2. **status** — read the container's native state and map it to the fixed vocabulary (`PENDING RUNNING COMPLETE ERROR CANCELED UNKNOWN`).\n3. **logs** — fetch the log tail by `$JOB_ID` and confirm the token appears.\n4. **cancel** — remove the container, mark the record CANCELED, and confirm the container is actually gone.\n\nPull the image as its own step before `docker run` rather than letting the run pull implicitly — on a billed GPU host an implicit first-time pull is charged as idle GPU time, and a separate pull also distinguishes an auth/entitlement failure from a training failure in the logs.\n\nNotes on the environment: the eval container has the host Docker socket mounted, so `docker` commands create sibling containers on the host — bind-mount paths must be ones the host daemon can resolve. Clean up the container you create; do not leave a job record stranded in PENDING.\n\nWrite a short report to {artifacts_dir}/eval_report.md covering: the JOB_ID, the command used for each verb, the observed state at each step, the mapped status vocabulary value, and confirmation that both the container and the record reached a terminal state.",
      "expected_outcome": "The eval succeeds when all four verbs are executed against the real Docker daemon and the report at {artifacts_dir}/eval_report.md shows: (1) the job record was opened with scripts/tao_job_record.py BEFORE `docker run`, and the container was named after the minted $JOB_ID — an id minted after launch, or a container named anything else, is a failure because the later verbs then cannot locate the job; (2) the record was marked RUNNING with --backend-ref set to the container id; (3) status reported the container's native state mapped to the fixed vocabulary rather than the raw docker string; (4) logs were fetched by $JOB_ID and contained the tao-smoke-alive token; (5) the container was removed and the record marked CANCELED, with both confirmed terminal — a record left in PENDING is a failure; (6) the image was pulled or confirmed present before `docker run` rather than pulled implicitly by it; (7) no GPU was requested and no TAO image was pulled, since this is a lifecycle check. Using the SDK (any tao_sdk import or *SDK() constructor) is a failure: this platform is SDK-free and drives docker directly.",
      "expected_behavior": []
    }
  ],
  "credentials": [],
  "plugins": {
    "claude": [
      {
        "marketplace": "${CI_PROJECT_DIR}",
        "plugin": "tao-skills@tao-skill-bank"
      }
    ],
    "codex": [
      {
        "marketplace": "${CI_PROJECT_DIR}",
        "plugin": "tao-skill-bank@tao-skill-bank"
      }
    ]
  }
}
